WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:02.443
- [Interviewer] Sorry about that.

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:04.990
All right, Brad.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:06.150
Brad, where are you from originally?

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:07.470
Where'd you grow up?

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:09.240
- Michigan, Detroit area.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.920
- Detroit?
- Suburbs.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.760
- [Interviewer] And how was your family?

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.030
You grew up with both your parents?

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:13.863
- Yeah.

00:00:14.844 --> 00:00:17.530
It was a great family,
I had a great childhood.

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:18.867
- [Interviewer] That's nice to hear.

00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:22.560
And after you left life
at home with your parents,

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:23.960
where did you go from there?

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:26.653
- I started traveling around.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:31.410
Different places.

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.253
Colorado, Boston,

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.370
Louisiana, and then I ended up in Florida,

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.090
and what was happening was,
I was drinking too much,

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.773
and I was an alcoholic, really.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.373
But I found AA, and I got sober.

00:00:48.270 --> 00:00:49.320
But I didn't stay sober.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.100
It lasted for about a year and a half.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.620
And then, I relapsed,
and I've been relapsing

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:55.720
ever since, basically.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:59.410
So I have good periods, but
then I have bad periods.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:01.510
- [Interviewer] Are you
using any of the drugs

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:02.610
that are out here in the street?

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.970
- Yeah, drugs are part of my story.

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:05.803
Yep.

00:01:05.803 --> 00:01:07.278
- [Interviewer] They're all
the same problem, basically.

00:01:07.278 --> 00:01:08.413
Alcohol or--
- I think so, yeah.

00:01:08.413 --> 00:01:10.330
- Drugs.
- Yeah, sure are.

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:12.060
- [Interviewer] Just different
forms of the same problem.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:12.943
I believe so.

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.274
Alcohol can be the worst drug.

00:01:16.274 --> 00:01:17.107
- [Interviewer] It's so easy.

00:01:17.107 --> 00:01:18.640
- It can really tear you up.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:19.717
I mean, look at 'em.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:22.230
- [Interviewer] So what age were you

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.223
when you started with drinking?

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:31.018
- When I knew I was an
alcoholic was when I was 35.

00:01:31.018 --> 00:01:32.080
- 35?
- Yeah.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.330
In Florida.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:34.540
And I got sober, like I said.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.180
For a year and a half,
everything was great.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.513
But then I drank.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.560
Then I got sober again, and then I drank.

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:43.760
And it's been like that ever since.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:45.110
And I'm 64 now.

00:01:45.110 --> 00:01:45.970
- [Interviewer] You're 64?

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:46.803
- Yeah.

00:01:48.856 --> 00:01:49.920
I was 55--
- How long you been

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:51.973
down here on Skid Row?

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:55.050
- About seven years.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.423
I don't remember exactly.

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:02.683
I have one of those SROs.

00:02:04.270 --> 00:02:09.270
It still counts as homeless
when they do the census,

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:11.800
but I've had one for about three years.

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:14.423
But I was on the streets
for three years, too.

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:18.593
- [Interviewer] And what is your drug now?

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:23.133
- A mixture of meth and crack.

00:02:26.475 --> 00:02:28.720
I try to stay away from the
crack, it's too expensive.

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:30.020
I don't really like crack.

00:02:31.490 --> 00:02:35.280
So, if I do meth, I don't do crack.

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:37.288
It's a lot cheaper.
- I've heard that story a lot.

00:02:37.288 --> 00:02:39.700
- Yeah, that's why--
- Meth seems to be a--

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:40.920
- Substitute.
- A way out of

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:41.810
crack addiction.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.750
- Yeah, and it's a way,

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.393
crack was a way out of drinking.

00:02:46.429 --> 00:02:47.840
And now, meth is a way out of crack.

00:02:47.840 --> 00:02:51.520
- [Interviewer] What do you
think the core problem is?

00:02:51.520 --> 00:02:55.791
'Cause drinking, meth,
crack, kind of symptoms of

00:02:55.791 --> 00:02:56.624
something deeper--
- I have an addictive

00:02:56.624 --> 00:02:59.403
personality, I think I'm
an alcoholic, basically.

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.130
- [Interviewer] But do
you believe that the,

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.910
the drive to numb some
painful event in your past

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:11.413
is what's been--
- It's not that.

00:03:11.413 --> 00:03:13.023
Alcohol is a tricky thing.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:18.010
I'm not trying to numb
some pain, I don't think.

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.396
Maybe I have--
- 'Cause your childhood

00:03:19.396 --> 00:03:21.038
seems pretty good.
- Huh?

00:03:21.038 --> 00:03:22.200
- [Interviewer] Your
childhood seemed pretty good.

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:24.190
- Yeah, it was wonderful.

00:03:24.190 --> 00:03:26.520
I just have that addictive personality.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:27.993
The dependence on something.

00:03:30.278 --> 00:03:33.120
I think it's, not everybody has it.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:34.053
Some people do.

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.910
There's a small percentage
of people who do.

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:40.710
And you either got it, or you don't.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.573
It's like a disease.
- Yeah.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:47.530
- Mental, physical, and spiritual.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.681
I think that's what my problem is.

00:03:49.681 --> 00:03:50.930
I know that's what it is.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:53.760
I've never been able
to stay sober, though.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.340
For longer than a,

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.110
the first time I got sober
was like, a year and a half,

00:03:59.110 --> 00:04:00.120
was the longest that was.

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:03.960
And after a year and a half,
everything was beautiful.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.700
Birds were chirping, and I was
happy, the sun was shining.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:10.523
And I felt great all the time.

00:04:11.566 --> 00:04:14.850
And I drank, and, (sighing)

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.870
I was drinking like, 18 beers a day.

00:04:17.870 --> 00:04:19.823
And I bought 24 beers every day.

00:04:20.920 --> 00:04:24.253
And going to work, I tore myself up.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:31.023
And then, I lost my job,
not related to drinking.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:33.503
And

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.230
I met a girl, real pretty
girl, I fell in love with her.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:41.170
And she smoked crack, and
I smoked, then I started,

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:43.947
and that was, it was it.

00:04:45.966 --> 00:04:48.960
And then, I smoked crack for a while,

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.609
and then it was meth, 'cause I wanted,

00:04:51.609 --> 00:04:53.690
I don't even like crack anymore.

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:54.813
Just overdoing it.

00:04:56.486 --> 00:04:57.323
And that's it.

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:01.438
- [Interviewer] Do you
have kids of your own?

00:05:01.438 --> 00:05:02.271
- [Brad] No.

00:05:02.271 --> 00:05:03.104
- [Interviewer] No kids?

00:05:03.104 --> 00:05:04.020
Never married?

00:05:04.020 --> 00:05:04.853
- [Brad] No.

00:05:04.853 --> 00:05:06.220
- [Interviewer] And where do you see,

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:07.290
what do you see for your future?

00:05:07.290 --> 00:05:09.993
If addiction is gonna
be a nagging problem.

00:05:11.106 --> 00:05:14.023
- [Brad] It's not really a problem.

00:05:16.445 --> 00:05:18.600
I'm 64, so I'm retired.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:20.973
I don't get much money
a month, but I get it.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:26.760
Next year, I'll be able to get Medicaid

00:05:26.760 --> 00:05:28.860
or Medicare, whatever it is.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.630
Medicaid, yeah.

00:05:30.630 --> 00:05:32.633
And Section 8 housing.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:36.670
And that's it, I don't have a future.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:37.920
That's my future, really.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:40.830
I enjoy life.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.010
- [Interviewer] You're happy?

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:43.320
- [Brad] Yeah.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.738
- [Interviewer] Do you have friends?

00:05:44.738 --> 00:05:46.360
- Oh, yeah.
- Yeah?

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:47.799
- Yeah.

00:05:47.799 --> 00:05:50.410
- [Interviewer] What was the
lowest point in your life?

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:51.243
- Lowest?
- Yeah.

00:05:51.243 --> 00:05:52.877
- Oh, I have many low points, many.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.793
I think it was, I was in Alabama
once, that was pretty low.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:08.820
I was hitchhiking around the country.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:12.343
And I was pretty young,
I was in my 20s still.

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.990
I would ride in freight trains.

00:06:16.990 --> 00:06:18.360
You know, you hop a train and you go.

00:06:18.360 --> 00:06:19.590
And I was just doing it for fun.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.780
I wasn't doing it to get anywhere.

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.275
Just something to do.

00:06:23.275 --> 00:06:26.880
But then, I got messed up in
it and I couldn't get out.

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:28.430
It's like I was trapped.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:30.203
And it got pretty bad.

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:33.990
- [Interviewer] Yeah,
addiction's a curse, isn't it?

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:35.343
- [Brad] Yeah, it sure is.

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:38.160
Sure is.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.140
- [Interviewer] All right.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.110
Well, thank you so much for talking to me

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:41.250
and sharing your story.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:42.083
- All right.

00:06:42.083 --> 00:06:42.916
- [Interviewer] I appreciate it.

00:06:42.916 --> 00:06:43.749
Good luck with everything.

00:06:43.749 --> 00:06:44.582
- Thank you.

